# /etc/vpn/bypass: things that always skip the VPN and use the normal connection.
# After editing:  vpn apply          (manual: ~/vpn-setup/MANUAL.md)
#
# One entry per line:
#   - a domain; it also covers all its subdomains        e.g. youtube.com
#   - an IP or IP range, for apps with built-in addresses  e.g. 91.108.4.0/22
# Works for every program (browsers, Flatpak apps, curl). If something is on both
# this list and /etc/vpn/vpn-only, the VPN-only list wins.
# Don't list huge shared domains (google.com, googleapis.com, cloudflare.com, amazonaws.com).

# --- YouTube (site + app)
youtube.com
youtu.be
youtube-nocookie.com
youtubei.googleapis.com
googlevideo.com
ytimg.com
ggpht.com

# --- Gmail
# Google serves many of its sites from shared addresses, so some other Google traffic
# may skip the VPN too. Sign-in is listed so Google sees you from one place only.
mail.google.com
gmail.com
accounts.google.com

# --- LinkedIn (site, its image/script server, short links)
linkedin.com
licdn.com
lnkd.in

# --- Telegram (app + site)
# The app connects to built-in IPs, so its official ranges are listed too:
# https://core.telegram.org/resources/cidr.txt (checked 2026-11-26)
telegram.org
telegram.me
t.me
telesco.pe
91.108.56.0/22
91.108.4.0/22
91.108.8.0/22
91.108.16.0/22
91.108.12.0/22
149.154.160.0/20
91.105.192.0/23
91.108.20.0/22
185.76.151.0/24
2001:b28:f23d::/48
2001:b28:f23f::/48
2001:67c:4e8::/48
2001:b28:f23c::/48
2a0a:f280::/32
